WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.799
This lesson covers what happens when retrying won't solve the problem.

2
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:09.439
We'll cover how to build fallback chains that provide ordered alternatives when the primary fails,

3
00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:14.640
how to pre-validate each step, how degradation signals travel downstream, and

4
00:00:15.039 --> 00:00:19.559
when to escalate instead of accepting a partial answer. Let's get into it.

5
00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:24.319
The primary path just failed and retrying isn't going to help.

6
00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:29.953
What the agent does in that moment is the whole subject here. A fallback chain is an ordered

7
00:00:29.953 --> 00:00:31.902
sequence of alternative strategies

8
00:00:32.479 --> 00:00:34.479
attempted when the primary action fails.

9
00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:41.939
Try tool A, then tool B, then a cached result, then escalate to human review. Each step is

10
00:00:42.119 --> 00:00:48.619
pre-planned, not improvised. Graceful degradation means returning a useful but incomplete result while

11
00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:54.304
explicitly signaling the reduced quality downstream, so the limitation is visible and the rest of

12
00:00:54.304 --> 00:00:56.043
the pipeline can respond to it.

13
00:00:56.700 --> 00:01:03.020
Silent degradation is the failure mode, where the agent returns a degraded result with no signal at all, and

14
00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:06.339
downstream treats it as full quality.

15
00:01:07.459 --> 00:01:13.779
A fallback chain is built as an ordered sequence of alternatives, where each step fires only if the previous one failed.

16
00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:21.459
The classic structure is, try the primary tool first. If that fails, try an alternative tool. If that also fails,

17
00:01:21.699 --> 00:01:26.459
try a cached result from a prior successful run. If that isn't available,

18
00:01:26.980 --> 00:01:32.059
escalate to a human reviewer. Each step in the chain has to be a pre-validated alternative,

19
00:01:32.180 --> 00:01:37.980
not something added in a moment of panic. The chain always terminates in an explicit outcome,

20
00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:42.819
either a result at whatever quality level was achievable, or a clear escalation.

21
00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:48.940
Fallback chains convert ad hoc failure handling into a systematic predictable process.

22
00:01:49.339 --> 00:01:54.160
That predictability is what makes them reliable under real production conditions.

23
00:01:55.779 --> 00:02:01.379
Pre-validation is the discipline that separates a real fallback chain from a false sense of safety. An

24
00:02:02.059 --> 00:02:09.100
untested fallback is a liability, not a safety net. Under the exact conditions that caused the primary to fail, an

25
00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:15.940
untested fallback may fail for identical reasons or introduce entirely new failure modes you didn't anticipate.

26
00:02:16.779 --> 00:02:21.940
Before deploying any fallback in production, validate that it works when the primary fails.

27
00:02:22.460 --> 00:02:27.660
Confirm that its output meets the minimum quality bar that downstream components require.

28
00:02:28.059 --> 00:02:35.779
Test it under realistic load and edge case inputs. A fallback that was never validated looks like insurance on paper.

29
00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:40.500
In practice, it behaves like a second point of failure, hiding behind the first one.

30
00:02:41.779 --> 00:02:47.478
Graceful and silent degradation aren't just stylistic choices. They have concrete consequences for

31
00:02:47.478 --> 00:02:48.641
how errors compound.

32
00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:54.300
Graceful degradation means the agent returns a partial or lower quality result and

33
00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:59.908
explicitly signals the degradation to every downstream component that receives it. The signal could

34
00:02:59.908 --> 00:03:00.847
be a flag field, a

35
00:03:01.059 --> 00:03:04.139
confidence score, or a status code in the output schema.

36
00:03:04.779 --> 00:03:11.419
Downstream logic reads the signal and adjusts. It might lower its own confidence, route to a human reviewer, or

37
00:03:11.699 --> 00:03:14.979
refuse to commit an irreversible action based on partial data.

38
00:03:15.899 --> 00:03:21.220
Silent degradation skips all of that. The agent returns a degraded result with no signal.

39
00:03:21.779 --> 00:03:27.583
Downstream treats it as authoritative and errors compound invisibly through the pipeline until they

40
00:03:27.583 --> 00:03:29.401
surface as a wrong final result

41
00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:32.539
that's nearly impossible to trace back to the original fallback.

42
00:03:33.539 --> 00:03:38.380
Degradation signals don't stay useful if they die at the component that triggered the fallback.

43
00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:44.154
They have to propagate downstream with the result, include a degradation flag, or metadata field

44
00:03:44.154 --> 00:03:45.921
directly in the output object.

45
00:03:46.660 --> 00:03:53.580
Every downstream component that receives the output should check that field before deciding how to treat the data.

46
00:03:54.059 --> 00:03:59.460
A component that sees a degradation flag can lower its confidence in the result, route it differently, or

47
00:04:00.179 --> 00:04:03.259
refuse to use it for an action that requires high reliability.

48
00:04:04.259 --> 00:04:10.245
Without that propagation, every component after the fallback is operating on a false assumption that

49
00:04:10.245 --> 00:04:12.280
it received a normal, full-quality

50
00:04:12.860 --> 00:04:19.540
output. That false assumption is what turns a contained fallback event into a pipeline-wide silent failure.

51
00:04:20.619 --> 00:04:24.459
Three anti-patterns show up repeatedly in fallback chain design.

52
00:04:24.619 --> 00:04:30.275
The first is deploying untested fallbacks, which introduces new failure modes exactly when the

53
00:04:30.275 --> 00:04:32.079
system most needs reliability.

54
00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:38.260
An untested fallback may fail for the same reason the primary did, or for a different reason.

55
00:04:38.540 --> 00:04:41.940
That's harder to diagnose because it sits inside a recovery path.

56
00:04:42.299 --> 00:04:48.140
The second is silent masking. A chain that returns a degraded result with no signal and

57
00:04:48.459 --> 00:04:51.899
forces every downstream component to treat it as full quality

58
00:04:52.700 --> 00:04:57.660
isn't graceful degradation. It is a failure mode pretending to be a recovery mechanism.

59
00:04:57.739 --> 00:05:03.700
The third is a chain with no terminal step, which can loop or stall instead of failing cleanly.

60
00:05:04.779 --> 00:05:09.500
When the primary action fails and fallbacks have run, the pipeline has a choice.

61
00:05:09.980 --> 00:05:13.459
Return a partial result or return nothing and escalate.

62
00:05:13.940 --> 00:05:20.059
Partial success means delivering a useful but incomplete result with an explicit degradation signal attached.

63
00:05:20.299 --> 00:05:26.579
It's valid when downstream components can handle incomplete data and the incompleteness doesn't cause harm.

64
00:05:26.899 --> 00:05:30.579
Full failure means returning nothing and triggering escalation.

65
00:05:30.779 --> 00:05:35.299
It's correct when a partial result would actually be worse than no result at all.

66
00:05:35.579 --> 00:05:40.220
The determining factor is whether a partial result causes downstream harm.

67
00:05:40.700 --> 00:05:43.339
A partially summarized document is usually fine.

68
00:05:43.859 --> 00:05:46.540
A partially validated payment instruction isn't.

69
00:05:46.859 --> 00:05:52.660
Know which situation you're designing for before choosing between partial success and full escalation.

70
00:05:53.579 --> 00:06:00.140
The decision between escalating and accepting a degraded output comes down to risk level and reversibility.

71
00:06:00.540 --> 00:06:05.100
For low-risk reversible actions, a degraded output is often acceptable.

72
00:06:05.700 --> 00:06:12.700
Propagate the signal, note the quality reduction, and let the pipeline continue with that context visible. For high-risk

73
00:06:13.019 --> 00:06:17.739
irreversible actions, a degraded output may produce harm that can't be undone.

74
00:06:18.220 --> 00:06:23.739
Sending an email, processing a payment, deleting a record. Those actions call for a higher threshold.

75
00:06:23.739 --> 00:06:30.739
If the best available result is degraded and the action is irreversible, escalate to a human before proceeding.

76
00:06:31.100 --> 00:06:38.339
The higher the consequence of a wrong answer, the lower the tolerance for accepting degraded output as a substitute.

77
00:06:39.339 --> 00:06:45.779
A fallback chain gives your system somewhere to go when the primary path is gone and retrying won't bring it back.

78
00:06:46.299 --> 00:06:49.619
That's what keeps a workflow answering instead of collapsing.

79
00:06:50.019 --> 00:06:55.779
One thing to watch, a fallback that fires quietly is worse than the outage it replaced because now

80
00:06:56.059 --> 00:07:00.019
everyone downstream is trusting a degraded answer at full confidence.

81
00:07:00.420 --> 00:07:03.380
That does it for this lesson. I'll see you in the next one.

